==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{m|en|Eid}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{alternative case form of|Eid|lang=en}}

===Etymology 2===
From English dialectal {{m|en|eid}}, from {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|eið||an isthmus, neck of land}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*aidiją||isthmus, strait}}, of uncertain origin, but probably from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₁y-||to go}}. Cognate with {{cog|is|eið}}, {{m|is|eiði}}, {{cog|fo|eið}}, {{m|fo|eiði||isthmus}}, {{cog|no|eid||isthmus}}, {{cog|sv|ed}}. Compare {{cog|la|eō||go, proceed|pos=verb}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ed}}, {{l|en|aith}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal|Scotland}} An [[isthmus]] or narrow neck of land jutting out into the sea; a [[sandbank]] cast up by the sea across the head of an open [[bight]] or inlet and having a lagoon inside it.

===Anagrams===
* {{l|en|die}}, {{l|en|EDI}}, {{l|en|ide}}, {{l|en|IDE}}, {{l|en|IED}}

